WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.460
Federal pressure on state election rules left only 55% of Democrats confident the midterm count will be accurate. This is Omni View, Episode 222. Dakar opens youth Olympic games on African soil, protesters gather at Poland's constitutional tribunal, and foreign ministers at a group of 20 meeting leave without a security deal, then a look at how American states run federal elections. Trump administration officials have pressed states to adopt new federal level checks, including the SAV Act,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.320
which would require documentary proof of United States citizenship to register or vote.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:41.200
France 24 reports those moves against a backdrop of repeated claims that large numbers of non-citizens are voting,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.560
even though records show such cases are exceedingly rare.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.280
United States elections are run by states under the Constitution,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.300
and citizenship is already required, with identification checks already in place.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:01:00.380
case. An AP Nork poll found 38% of Republicans confident the final count will be accurate. Non-citizen

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.540
voting is already subject to fines, prison, or deportation. The Heritage Foundation

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:14.900
database lists 100 cases of non-citizen voting from 1982 to 2025, representing roughly 0.0008%

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.520
of more than 1.3 billion votes cast in presidential elections in that period.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.180
The United States does not issue a national identity card, so driver's licenses and other

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.360
state documents would not meet the proposed standard unless they record place of birth.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.020
The same reporting notes attempts to shape nearly every stage of the process ahead of

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.480
the November 3rd vote, and Trump's past challenges to results.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.720
Jonathan Diaz of the Campaign Legal Center observed that the administration has tried to insert

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.840
the federal government into almost every aspect of the election process, creating an atmosphere

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.120
fear of fear and uncertainty even when changes have not succeeded.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.700
Think tanks aligned with the administration's goals have themselves found no evidence

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:56.840
of widespread fraud.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.180
Citizenship is already required.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:04.160
States already administer the vote, and penalties for unlawful attempts already sit on

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:04.580
the books.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.180
The fight is over whether extra documents should be added on top.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.280
Advocates for the SAVE Act maintain that explicit documentary proof closes even small vulnerabilities

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:23.800
and a system where public trust is already divided along party lines, and that doing so would strengthen the perceived legitimacy of outcomes without changing who is eligible to vote.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:35.200
They argue the requirement lines up with basic verification used in other official processes, and that the current patchwork of state rules leaves room for doubt that only a uniform federal standard can resolve.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.820
Their own case treats the gap as one of proof rather than missing punishments.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:44.720
Diaz and the Campaign Legal Center argue the rule adds a barrier where none is needed,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.440
because states already enforce citizenship through existing identification systems.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.880
That view holds that many lawful voters would have to obtain new paperwork, risking lower

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.060
participation among eligible citizens who lack ready access to the specific documents required.

00:03:01.003 --> 00:03:06.903
Dakar has opened the 2026 Youth Olympic Games, the first Olympics hosted on African soil,

00:03:06.903 --> 00:03:14.043
Approximately 2,700 athletes aged 14-17 are competing across 25 sports through November

00:03:14.843 --> 00:03:15.483
13th.

00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:19.983
New sports, including baseball, five, Wushu, and beach wrestling appear for the first

00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:20.283
time.

00:03:20.823 --> 00:03:26.063
And athletes will contest 153 medal events, with some sports making their youth Olympic

00:03:26.063 --> 00:03:26.543
debut.

00:03:27.103 --> 00:03:33.423
The mascot IO, a young lion in a traditional Fulani hat whose name means joy in Yoruba, features

00:03:33.423 --> 00:03:38.463
in opening ceremonies that include hundreds of dancers celebrating Senegalese and African culture.

00:03:38.963 --> 00:03:44.603
President Basirou Diyomai-Fey is scheduled to declare the games open at Diamniadio Stadium,

00:03:45.143 --> 00:03:50.043
where International Olympic Committee, or IOC, President Kirsti Coventry and several

00:03:50.043 --> 00:03:55.923
heads of state are expected. The games were originally planned for 2022 but postponed four years

00:03:55.923 --> 00:04:01.723
by the coronavirus pandemic. The IOC is treating Dakar as a test of whether the continent can host

00:04:01.723 --> 00:04:07.203
at this scale. And student Thomas DMA described the event as a first opportunity for Senegal

00:04:07.203 --> 00:04:13.503
in Africa to discover new sports and welcome other cultures. French actor Omar Sye and Senegalese

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:20.203
Italian tiktok star Kaby Lamb serve as ambassadors. Organizers will monitor participation numbers

00:04:20.203 --> 00:04:24.503
and infrastructure used to assess whether future events can be staged on the continent.

00:04:25.514 --> 00:04:30.234
Demonstrators assembled outside Poland's constitutional tribunal after the government suspended

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:35.494
the court's president, a step opponents call unlawful, and Poland's president cut short

00:04:35.494 --> 00:04:37.314
that Senegal trip to stand with them.

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:43.134
AP News reports the head of state joined the gathering, underscoring tension between the executive

00:04:43.134 --> 00:04:49.234
and the tribunal, which reviews the constitutionality of laws. Police increased security around the

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:53.434
building and detained several people after some protesters breached the perimeter fence.

00:04:54.034 --> 00:04:58.794
Everything turns on whether the government had lawful authority to suspend the tribunal's president,

00:04:58.794 --> 00:05:02.014
or whether that act crosses into the court's independence.

00:05:02.694 --> 00:05:06.574
Supporters of the suspension maintain the government acted within its authority to

00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:11.194
address perceived irregularities at the top of an institution whose decisions affect

00:05:11.194 --> 00:05:14.214
national law, preserving democratic accountability.

00:05:14.854 --> 00:05:20.294
They acknowledge the risk of appearing to politicize the judiciary, but argue oversight is necessary

00:05:20.294 --> 00:05:25.474
when public trust in the court is low because the tribunals ruling shape legislation across

00:05:25.474 --> 00:05:30.874
many policy areas, and accountability mechanisms must remain available. Critics

00:05:30.874 --> 00:05:34.874
contend that removing the president without a clear legal process undermines a

00:05:34.874 --> 00:05:39.294
core safeguard in Poland's constitutional system, and that such moves erode

00:05:39.294 --> 00:05:44.174
checks on executive power regardless of the stated rationale. They emphasize that a

00:05:44.174 --> 00:05:48.794
court which reviews statutes needs insulation from direct political pressure if

00:05:48.794 --> 00:05:53.671
it is to function as intended. European ministers confronted Russia's Sergei

00:05:53.671 --> 00:05:59.191
Lavrov over Ukraine as the group of 20 or G-20 foreign ministers meeting in the United

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:03.831
States ended two days of talks without consensus on several global security matters.

00:06:04.311 --> 00:06:07.951
Those European ministers declined to join the traditional group photo.

00:06:08.591 --> 00:06:13.371
On the sidelines, United States Secretary of State Marco Rubio signed an Arctic cooperation

00:06:13.371 --> 00:06:18.871
deal with Japan and South Korea and confirmed Washington's participation in Riyadh's World

00:06:18.871 --> 00:06:20.071
Expo in 2030.

00:06:20.071 --> 00:06:25.291
The ministers were unable to reach joint positions on multiple security topics despite

00:06:25.291 --> 00:06:30.391
the two days of discussions, even as the United States pursued those separate bilateral steps.

00:06:30.891 --> 00:06:35.051
Follow-up diplomatic channels will determine whether any working-level agreements emerged

00:06:35.051 --> 00:06:36.571
before the next-liter summit.

00:06:37.572 --> 00:06:42.452
Oil firms posted higher earnings as prices rose amid conflict-related supply concerns,

00:06:42.932 --> 00:06:46.012
and pump prices for gasoline and diesel increased accordingly.

00:06:46.012 --> 00:06:51.952
NPR links the profit surge to higher crude prices driven by the war in Iran, and to the

00:06:51.952 --> 00:06:53.752
resulting pressure at filling stations.

00:06:54.392 --> 00:06:58.952
The war in Iran has contributed to the upward pressure on crude benchmarks, connecting

00:06:58.952 --> 00:07:03.992
the quarterly results to broader supply dynamics and to what drivers pay at retail fuel outlets.

00:07:04.692 --> 00:07:09.152
Companies will release detailed production and investment figures in coming weeks that

00:07:09.152 --> 00:07:12.272
will show how much of the revenue is directed toward new capacity.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:18.032
Inflation trends and growth data left several central banks holding policy rates unchanged

00:07:18.032 --> 00:07:19.372
in their latest decisions.

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:24.132
Bloomberg tracks that coordinated pause and notes that the same inflation and growth

00:07:24.132 --> 00:07:26.932
reading supported the choices in multiple jurisdictions.

00:07:27.752 --> 00:07:33.132
Markets had largely anticipated the moves, and the pauses were widely expected by market participants.

00:07:33.792 --> 00:07:38.392
Traders will watch upcoming inflation prints and labor data for any signal of a shift in

00:07:38.392 --> 00:07:39.112
the coming months.

00:07:39.112 --> 00:07:44.234
Chesapeake Bay residents and researchers are pushing in the right direction by restoring

00:07:44.234 --> 00:07:48.494
oyster reefs after over-harvesting reduced numbers and harmed water quality.

00:07:49.314 --> 00:07:54.134
NPR describes volunteers and scientists rebuilding oyster populations through farming and

00:07:54.134 --> 00:07:59.534
habitat projects and notes measurable improvements in localized water conditions where reefs have

00:07:59.534 --> 00:08:00.554
been reestablished.

00:08:01.174 --> 00:08:06.334
Oyster reefs once supported broader marine life in the Bay and they once abounded in the Chesapeake

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:11.014
before overharvesting cut their populations and affected both water quality and habitat

00:08:11.014 --> 00:08:11.974
for other species.

00:08:12.594 --> 00:08:17.274
Project leaders will track oyster survival rates and water quality measurements over the

00:08:17.274 --> 00:08:19.414
next two seasons to evaluate expansion.

00:08:20.407 --> 00:08:24.547
To really understand a federal voting rule, you have to follow the split of labor in the

00:08:24.547 --> 00:08:25.247
constitution.

00:08:25.847 --> 00:08:30.727
Washington may set limited conditions, but each state still builds the machinery that puts

00:08:30.727 --> 00:08:32.067
a ballot in someone's hand.

00:08:32.067 --> 00:08:37.267
The common misunderstanding is that the federal government can simply take over election administration.

00:08:37.847 --> 00:08:44.787
It cannot, in the ordinary sense of running the poll books, designing the ballot, choosing polling places, or counting the votes.

00:08:45.287 --> 00:08:47.867
Those jobs belong to the states, and they always have.

00:08:48.567 --> 00:08:50.227
That arrangement dates to the founding.

00:08:50.707 --> 00:08:56.907
Each state sets its own voter registration rules, ballot design, polling locations, and counting procedures,

00:08:56.907 --> 00:09:01.467
subject only to federal laws that protect voting rights and prevent discrimination.

00:09:02.127 --> 00:09:06.607
When Congress passes a voting law, states must still enact and administer the change through

00:09:06.607 --> 00:09:07.507
their own systems.

00:09:08.107 --> 00:09:12.587
A federal proposal can add conditions that states have to implement without actually transferring

00:09:12.587 --> 00:09:14.587
the running of elections to federal officials.

00:09:15.267 --> 00:09:17.167
The practical test is in the text.

00:09:17.647 --> 00:09:20.787
Does the language direct states to update their registration databases?

00:09:21.447 --> 00:09:23.687
And which state officials would have to carry that out?

00:09:23.687 --> 00:09:28.927
Court. Earlier federal voting laws followed the same path. Congress wrote requirements and

00:09:28.927 --> 00:09:34.107
state officers had to rewrite forms, retrain clerks, and adjust local procedures before

00:09:34.107 --> 00:09:39.527
anything changed at a precinct. Knowing that history calibrates the present, the last several

00:09:39.527 --> 00:09:44.807
times Washington set a new voting condition, the visible work landed on state election offices,

00:09:45.247 --> 00:09:50.107
not on a national elections bureau, because no such bureau runs American polling places.

00:09:50.107 --> 00:09:55.467
When the next federal voting proposal appears, read the clause that tells states how to update

00:09:55.467 --> 00:09:59.947
their registration databases, and note which state officials would have to carry it out.

00:10:00.879 --> 00:10:05.759
France 24 starts from the administration's attempts to shape each stage of the vote, while

00:10:05.759 --> 00:10:10.639
the AP-NORK poll release isolates how confident each party's voters say they are in the count,

00:10:11.119 --> 00:10:14.059
including the question wording and sample behind those figures.

00:10:14.779 --> 00:10:19.179
Upcoming inflation prints and labor market reports will be the next readings used to judge

00:10:19.179 --> 00:10:22.259
whether those unchanged policy rates still fit the economy.

